            <ArticleTitle>Bioresorbable scaffolds for the treatment of in-stent restenosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>265-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00380-013-0464-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Percutaneous coronary intervention with bare-metal stents or drug-eluting stents can decrease clinical event rates compared with simple balloon angioplasty. However, stent implantation is often associated with subsequent restenosis. Bioresorbable coronary scaffolds provide short-term vessel scaffolding with drug delivery capability and are designed to avoid the long-term limitations of metallic stents such as late stent thrombosis and in-stent restenosis. To the best of our knowledge, we report the first case series of successful treatment of in-stent restenosis using bioresorbable scaffolds.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
